clusters.verdict_basis:
The audio exists. Nick Sortor and Rhein Amacher posted a 47-second clip on Oct. 11. In it, a woman who says she is at the Lindner Center of Hope in Mason, Ohio, says Talarico was "just discharged this evening" (
Yahoo/Yahoo News transcript
; 
Rolling Stone
). The claim says the audio supports the report, and the reporting does not bear that out. 
Snopes says both Talarico and the Lindner Center denied he was ever treated there
, and it rated the rumor false. 
PolitiFact reports that the center said Talarico has never received treatment from it, and that Talarico said he has "never even been to Ohio."
 PolitiFact also says the timeline doesn't fit. Fuller recordings show Amacher posing as a relative and giving a made-up "code" (
PolitiFact
; 
Rolling Stone
). So the clip is real, but it doesn't corroborate the report. "Purportedly" covers the call's origin, but "supporting the report" implies corroboration that the facility's denial and the fact-checks undercut.
